{
  "answer": "The encoding declaration must match the regular expression `^[ \\t\\f]*#.*?coding[:=][ \\t]*([-_.a-zA-Z0-9]+)`.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "^[ \\t\\f]*#.*?coding[:=][ \\t]*([-_.a-zA-Z0-9]+)"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "The `[build-system]` table in pyproject.toml declares the build system.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "The `[build-system]` table is used to store build-related data."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Using `from __future__ import annotations` makes all annotations in a module be stored as string literals instead of being evaluated at definition time, effectively postponing their evaluation.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "turning *all* annotations in a given module into string literals"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 3,
      "quote": "Postponing the evaluation of annotations solves both problems."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Context variables provide a way to store and retrieve data that is local to a logical execution context—such as request data, language settings, profiling, tracing, or logging—so that this information can be accessed across asynchronous calls without having to pass it explicitly.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "and request data in web applications, language context for `gettext`, etc."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "The with statement requires a context manager to implement the __enter__() and __exit__() methods.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "context managers provide `__enter__()` and `__exit__()` methods that are invoked on entry to and exit from the body of the with statement."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Under PEP 604 an optional type is expressed as the union of the type and None, e.g. `T | None`.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "As a shorthand for `Union[T1, None]` you can write `Optional[T1]`;"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "writing `Union[X, Y]` as `X | Y`"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "An enumeration is a distinct type created by subclassing Enum that defines a set of named constant members, each with a name and a value.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "we call `Color` an *enumeration* (or *enum*)"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "The properties of an enumeration are useful for defining an immutable, related set of constant values"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "PEP 585 lets you use the built‑in list type with subscription syntax (e.g., list[int]) as a generic type, replacing the need for typing.List.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "* `list`  # typing.List"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Underscores may be used after a base specifier and between digits in a numeric literal, but only a single underscore can appear consecutively and it must be placed between digits.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "The current proposal is to allow one underscore between digits, and after base specifiers in numeric literals."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 5,
      "quote": "Only one consecutive underscore allowed, and only between digits."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "The release segment is a series of one or more dot‑separated numeric components, each a non‑negative integer, expressed as `N(.N)*`.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "* Release segment: `N(.N)*`"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "All numeric components MUST be non-negative integers represented as sequences of ASCII digits."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "A virtual environment is a lightweight, isolated Python environment that includes its own Python binary and site‑packages directory, optionally isolated from system site directories while sharing the standard library with the base Python installation.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "This PEP proposes to add to Python a mechanism for lightweight \"virtual environments\" with their own site directories, optionally isolated from system site directories.  Each virtual environment has its own Python binary ... and shares the standard library with the base installed Python."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 3,
      "quote": "Thus, a Python virtual environment in its simplest form would consist of nothing more than a copy or symlink of the Python binary accompanied by a `pyvenv.cfg` file and a site-packages directory."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "The @dataclass decorator adds generated dunder methods to the class, most notably an __init__ method, a __repr__ method, and an __eq__ method (and, depending on options, ordering and hash methods).",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "- `init`: If true (the default), a `__init__` method will be generated."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "- `repr`: If true (the default), a `__repr__` method will be generated."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "- `eq`: If true (the default), an `__eq__` method will be generated."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "Using these fields, the decorator adds generated method definitions to the class to support instance initialization, a repr, comparison methods, and optionally other methods as described in the Specification_ section."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "In the DB‑API, paramstyle is a string constant that specifies the type of parameter‑marker formatting the module expects.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "String constant stating the type of parameter marker formatting expected by the interface."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "A protocol class is a class that inherits from `typing.Protocol`, serving as an interface definition whose required members (methods and variables) must be implemented by other classes.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "Protocols are defined by including a special new class `typing.Protocol` (an instance of `abc.ABCMeta`) in the base classes list, typically at the end of the list."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "Protocol classes should generally not have many method implementations, as they describe an interface, not an implementation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "A build backend is the Python object named by the `build-backend` entry in a project's `pyproject.toml`; it is a module (or a module:object) that implements the build backend interface and is imported and invoked to perform the build.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "`build-backend` is a string naming a Python object that will be used to perform the build"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "And we import `module_path` and then lookup `module_path.object_path` (or just `module_path` if `object_path` is missing)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "PEP 448 generalises the `*` and `**` unpacking operators so they can be used in many more places – allowing an arbitrary number of unpackings in function calls and permitting unpacking inside tuple, list, set, and dictionary displays.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "This PEP proposes extended usages of the `*` iterable unpacking operator and `**` dictionary unpacking operators to allow unpacking in more positions, an arbitrary number of times, and in function calls and displays."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "A namespace package is a new type of package that can be built from portions located in different directories or distributions, does not contain an __init__.py file, and has no __file__ attribute, with its __path__ being a read‑only iterable that aggregates the locations of its portions.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "This PEP defines a new type of package, the \"namespace package\"."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "* Namespace packages have no `__init__.py` module."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "* Portions of namespace packages need not all come from the same directory structure, or even from the same loader."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "An f-string is a string literal prefixed with 'f' (or 'F') that can contain expressions inside braces, which are evaluated at runtime and replaced with their values.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "In Python source code, an f-string is a literal string, prefixed with 'f', which contains expressions inside braces."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 3,
      "quote": "In source code, f-strings are string literals that are prefixed by the letter 'f' or 'F'."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "PEP 465 adds the `@` infix operator for matrix multiplication.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "This PEP proposes a new binary operator to be used for matrix multiplication, called `@`."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 2,
      "quote": "`@`    Same as `*`             `__matmul__`, `__rmatmul__`"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "The `yield from` expression delegates to another iterable, running its iterator to exhaustion while forwarding any values it yields or receives directly to or from the caller of the delegating generator, and if the sub‑iterator is a generator its `return` value becomes the value of the `yield from` expression.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "The iterator is run to exhaustion, during which time it yields and receives values directly to or from the caller of the generator containing the `yield from` expression (the \"delegating generator\")."
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "when the iterator is another generator, the subgenerator is allowed to execute a `return` statement with a value, and that value becomes the value of the `yield from` expression."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "A match statement evaluates the subject expression to get a subject value and then compares that value against the patterns in the case blocks.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "chunk": 1,
      "quote": "The resulting subject value is then used to select the first case block whose patterns succeeds matching it"
    },
    {
      "chunk": 4,
      "quote": "The pattern matching process takes as input a pattern (following `case`) and a subject value (following `match`)."
    }
  ]
}
